{"name": "fives_readable_multiples", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of the incumbent `prime_over_odd_reading`, with everything in it kept: primitive-built rules with a per-primitive cost, the final-digit discount, costs on powers/cubes and squares, times-table-graded multiples, the single preference moving plausibility from \"odd\" to \"prime\", Erlang ranges with a bonus for whole hundred-chart rows, the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment. The one change is that \"multiples of 5\" is unlike the other times tables: membership can be read straight off the numeral (it ends in 0 or 5), and counting by fives is a drilled skill, so the rule gets its own fitted prior bonus. So after sets such as 90, 100, 5, people commit to \"multiples of 5\" and say yes to 15, 25, 35 ... 95 almost always and no to 16, 92 or 93, more sharply than the incumbent predicts, while sets that fit other times tables are unaffected.", "context": "experiment2 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent prime_over_odd_reading"}
{"name": "fives_readable_multiples", "outcome": "dropped", "detail": "fold 0 too slow to fit: the fit of 'fives_readable_multiples' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of the incumbent `prime_over_odd_reading`, with everything in it kept: primitive-built rules with a per-primitive cost, the final-digit discount, costs on powers/cubes and squares, times-table-graded multiples, the single preference moving plausibility from \"odd\" to \"prime\", Erlang ranges with a bonus for whole hundred-chart rows, the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment. The one change is that \"multiples of 5\" is unlike the other times tables: membership can be read straight off the numeral (it ends in 0 or 5), and counting by fives is a drilled skill, so the rule gets its own fitted prior bonus. So after sets such as 90, 100, 5, people commit to \"multiples of 5\" and say yes to 15, 25, 35 ... 95 almost always and no to 16, 92 or 93, more sharply than the incumbent predicts, while sets that fit other times tables are unaffected.", "context": "experiment2 round 3"}

